   <define name="progressionTrope">
      <element name="progressionTrope">
         <a:documentation xmlns:a="http://relaxng.org/ns/compatibility/annotations/1.0">(Progression Trope) A generally recognizable [progression] device or [level design] convention [appearing in the footage]. [Progression] tropes rely on culturally mediated expectations to expedite [advancement, acknowledge attempts to advance, or afford players with a sense of progress, achievement, or productivity]. When overused or oversimplified, tropes often come at the expense of fair and inclusive representations (Lee et al., VGMS 4.2, 2024).This element will typically be repeated. It differs from the Narrative Trope element, which focuses on diegetic events and actions, by focusing on devices and conventions that influence or shape players’ decision-making. These devices and conventions often prompt non-diegetic actions.See TV Tropes for definitions and examples of many tropes in this controlled vocabulary. The use of TV Tropes should be accompanied by peer-reviewed scholarship in Media and Game Studies.Controlled vocabulary adapted, with changes and additions, from Lee et al., Controlled Vocabulary for Tropes, 2024, and TV Tropes 2026.</a:documentation>
         <choice>
            <value>Ability required to proceed</value>
            <a:documentation xmlns:a="http://relaxng.org/ns/compatibility/annotations/1.0"/>
            <value>All or nothing / zero-sum game</value>
            <a:documentation xmlns:a="http://relaxng.org/ns/compatibility/annotations/1.0"/>
            <value>Anti-idling</value>
            <a:documentation xmlns:a="http://relaxng.org/ns/compatibility/annotations/1.0"/>
